Tone Mapping Algorithm Design 2026

The tone mapping method converts high-bit-depth linear RGB into display-ready sRGB through two stages. Stage 1 performs adaptive exposure compensation and tone mapping at low resolution. Stage 2 combines this tone guidance with full-resolution image features to refine local contrast and detail. Training uses paired camera data and generative-model-assisted targets for the target sensor. Qualitative assessment is generally satisfactory, while balancing shadow visibility with highlight gradation in challenging HDR scenes remains the main limitation.

2.9.1 Background

In a mobile image signal processor (ISP), tone mapping converts high-bit-depth linear RGB into an sRGB image for standard dynamic range display. It compresses the captured luminance range while preserving natural appearance and visible detail, addressing the tone reproduction problem studied in photographic rendering [1].

Sensor-specific tone mapping requires attention to both input exposure and target appearance. Sensor response and upstream automatic exposure (AE) and ISP processing affect the linear RGB distribution, while training targets determine the brightness, contrast, and color that the network learns to reproduce. The method addresses these factors through exposure compensation in the low-resolution stage and training supervision matched to the target sensor.

Efficient learned enhancement methods such as HDRNet combine low-resolution prediction with full-resolution image processing [2]. The architecture described here uses low-resolution RGB tone guidance and a full-resolution refinement network.

2.9.2 Purpose

The objective is to produce display-ready sRGB images with natural overall brightness, consistent color, and visible local detail across normally exposed, backlit, and low-light scenes. Subject brightness should remain appropriate relative to the background, and shadow enhancement should preserve highlight gradation.

Stage 1 handles adaptive exposure compensation and low-resolution brightness and color mapping. Stage 2 refines local contrast and detail at full resolution. Generated training targets provide appearance supervision under the target sensor's imaging conditions.

2.9.3 Challenges

1 Exposure variation across scenes and sensors

The network must accommodate changes in input exposure without making the output excessively bright, dark, flat, or gray. Exposure compensation must support tone mapping without being treated as a complete correction for sensor-dependent color response, noise, or upstream ISP differences.

2 Shadow and highlight balance

Backlit subjects and bright backgrounds require different local adjustments. Raising overall brightness can weaken highlight gradation, while strong highlight compression can leave faces and other dark subjects insufficiently visible. The network must coordinate these competing requirements within the same image.

3 Detail and color preservation

Enhancement should preserve skin texture, cloud structure, and fine edges while avoiding halos, amplified noise, color shifts, and excessive smoothing. Small faces and colored illumination are particularly demanding because their local appearance can be poorly represented at low resolution.

4 Processing speed

The deployment target is below 100 ms for a 3000 x 4000 image on the target Qualcomm GPU, including all Stage 1 and Stage 2 processing.

2.9.4 Dataset Setup

Training uses in-house paired data, generated targets for the target sensor, and auxiliary data for detail enhancement.

1 In-house paired data

The in-house dataset pairs linear RGB images derived from iPhone ProRAW captures with the corresponding camera-rendered images of the same scenes.

Input: 16-bit linear RGB images derived from ProRAW captures.

Target: 8-bit sRGB images prepared from the corresponding camera-rendered JPG or HEIC files through consistent decoding and color conversion.

Stage 1 supervision: input-target pairs downsampled to one-eighth of the original width and height.

Stage 2 supervision: full-resolution pairs for the final linear-RGB-to-sRGB mapping.

Pixel-level supervision requires spatial correspondence between each input and target.

2 Generated targets for the target sensor

Sensor adaptation samples pair linear RGB inputs from the target sensor with enhanced sRGB targets produced offline by a large generative image model. The data construction draws on GGT-100K, which generates high-quality restoration targets from real-world images [3]. For tone mapping, the intended enhancements concern brightness, contrast, and color appearance while preserving scene content.

Before inclusion in pixel-level training, generated targets must be checked for alignment, scene-content consistency, suitable shadow and highlight rendering, and visible artifacts. Samples with altered geometry, invented structures, or incompatible texture should be excluded from direct pixel-level supervision. Generated targets are pseudo-ground truth, and their provenance should remain distinguishable from that of the original paired targets.

The generative model is used only for offline data preparation. Inference uses the two-stage tone mapping network.

3 Auxiliary detail augmentation

Blur, noise, and related degradations are applied to in-house sRGB targets to form degraded-sRGB-to-original-sRGB pairs for auxiliary detail enhancement. Because these pairs are constructed in the sRGB domain, their use in Stage 2 training must account for the difference from the main linear RGB input domain. The main paired data supervise the final linear-RGB-to-sRGB mapping.

2.9.5 Solution Design

The cascaded network uses Stage 1 to establish tone and color guidance at low resolution and Stage 2 to refine local contrast and detail at full resolution. Global adaptive exposure compensation operates within Stage 1. Stage 2 receives the full-resolution linear RGB input and fuses its encoder features with the Stage 1 output.

1 Stage 1 Global adaptive exposure compensation and tone mapping

Stage 1 receives the linear RGB input at one-eighth of the original width and height. An image-dependent global compensation component adjusts image-level brightness to reduce sensitivity to input exposure variations. The compensation operates on the captured image and does not alter sensor exposure settings.

A U-Net [4] performs the low-resolution mapping into the target tonal domain, including dynamic range compression and color adjustment. The compensation component provides an image-level adjustment, while the U-Net predicts an RGB image with spatially varying tone information.

The Stage 1 output is supervised by the downsampled target and passed to Stage 2 at one-eighth resolution. The 8-bit target format describes the supervision and delivery images, rather than the precision of intermediate network tensors.

2 Stage 2 Local contrast and detail enhancement

Stage 2 uses a U-Net encoder-decoder with skip connections [4] to refine local contrast and preserve fine detail. Its encoder receives the full-resolution linear RGB input and progressively extracts features through downsampling. At the one-eighth-resolution bottleneck, these features are concatenated with the low-resolution RGB output of Stage 1. The decoder reconstructs the full-resolution sRGB output from the fused features and skip connections.

This arrangement provides Stage 2 with tone guidance from Stage 1 and image features from the full-resolution linear RGB input. Full-resolution target images supervise the final output.

3 Training supervision

Both stages use L1 reconstruction loss, a pixelwise objective studied in neural image restoration [5]. Stage 1 compares its prediction with the downsampled target, and Stage 2 compares the final prediction with the full-resolution target. In-house samples use paired camera-rendered targets; target-sensor adaptation samples use accepted generated targets.

Reconstruction error against generated targets measures agreement with the selected appearance supervision. It does not independently establish fidelity to a captured ground-truth reference or perceptual quality.

2.9.6 Experiment Results

1 Current qualitative assessment

Current qualitative results are generally satisfactory overall. The main unresolved issue occurs in challenging HDR scenes: dark subjects and shadow regions are not always enhanced sufficiently while maintaining natural highlight gradation. These observations describe overall rendering and do not isolate the contribution of either exposure compensation or generated training targets.

2 Validation priorities

Validation should use held-out captures to assess overall appearance and region-level shadow visibility, highlight gradation, skin rendering, and artifacts. Ablation tests should evaluate the contributions of generated training targets and exposure compensation under the same evaluation conditions. Latency must be measured at the target resolution on the target GPU to establish whether the network meets the deployment budget.

2.9.7 Limitations

1 Spatially competing tone requirements

Global compensation provides an image-level adjustment, but a bright background and a dark foreground can require different changes. Resolving that conflict depends on the spatial tone mapping and local refinement stages. The current HDR shadow-highlight imbalance shows that this remains an open limitation.

2 Information lost before tone mapping

Exposure compensation cannot restore scene detail lost through sensor saturation or upstream clipping. Raising dark regions also cannot remove the uncertainty caused by low signal-to-noise ratio. Improvements must be assessed against the information actually present in the input.

3 Complex illumination and small subjects

Color under non-white illumination, skin on small faces, and foreground-background contrast require continued validation. Low-resolution tone guidance may not adequately represent these regions, increasing the demands on Stage 2.

4 Reliability of generated supervision

Generated targets can introduce content changes, artificial texture, or inconsistent tonal preferences. Such errors can become training bias even when reconstruction loss is low. Filtering unsuitable targets and validating on independent captures are necessary to assess whether adaptation improves the intended rendering.

